# A value too deep for the C stack is COMPARED without one (panel 076) — the
# other half of `fixedbugs-a-deep-value-released-without-a-deep-stack.hero`.
#
# **The symptom was the worst this language can have**: exit 139, no output, no
# diagnostic, no `hero_panic` line. `record Node { label: i64, children: [Node] }`
# is the shape the specification itself invites — *"`children: [Node]` is a
# tree"* — and `==` is documented as structural *"recursively"*, with no bound. A
# reader who composes those two sentences got a segmentation fault.
#
# **Cause**: `hero_array_eq` called the element's `eq`, the generated `_eq` called
# back into `hero_array_eq`, one C frame per level. The cliff was ~52 200 at `-O0`
# — and it is **not a property of the program**: the same binary at the same depth
# exits 0 in a plain environment and 139 with one 900 KB environment variable set,
# because the environment is copied onto the stack at process start. `ulimit`
# moves it too, and `-O2` moves it 2.6x, which means `heroes run` hid what
# `heroes build` showed. That is why panel 076 refused a depth number outright.
#
# **Fix, 2026-08-16**: `runtime/parts/array.c` defers. `true` is the identity of
# `&&`, so a nested `hero_array_eq` pushes its elementwise work, answers `true`
# provisionally, and the outermost call drains the queue and ANDs the real answer
# in. Zero lines in the compiler, so the Heroes port reproduces nothing.
#
# **What this case pins is CORRECTNESS, not merely survival**, because a wrong
# `==` would be worse than a crash. The second comparison differs only at the
# DEEPEST level — the last thing a LIFO drain reaches — and the nested-array pair
# checks that an ordinary shallow answer did not change.
#
# Depth 60000 sits just past the measured -O0 cliff and keeps the case fast; the
# fix was verified separately at 1 000 000.
#
# Adversarial case — UNVERIFIED, pending debrief.

record Node
    label: i64
    children: [Node]

function chain(depth: i64, tip: i64) -> Node
    n: Node @ Node(label: tip, children: [])
    i: i64 @ 0
    while i < depth
        n @ Node(label: i, children: [n])
        i @ i + 1
    return n

function main()
    a = chain(depth: 60000, tip: 0)
    b = chain(depth: 60000, tip: 0)
    print(a == b)
    c = chain(depth: 60000, tip: 9)
    print(a == c)
    print([[1, 2], [3]] == [[1, 2], [3]])
    print([[1, 2], [3]] == [[1, 2], [4]])
